00:00:06.839 --> 00:00:07.630
SANDY JONES: Hello.

00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:09.610
Welcome to "Google Cloud OnAir."

00:00:09.610 --> 00:00:12.490
It's a live webinar series
hosted by Google Cloud.

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:14.544
We're hosting these
webinars every Tuesday.

00:00:14.544 --> 00:00:16.210
My name is Sandy
Jones, and today, we're

00:00:16.210 --> 00:00:18.075
going to talk about
Hangouts Meet Hardware.

00:00:18.075 --> 00:00:20.502
You can ask questions at
any time using the platform.

00:00:20.502 --> 00:00:22.960
And we have Googlers standing
by to answer those questions.

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:25.431
As well, I'll handle a few of
them at the end the session.

00:00:25.431 --> 00:00:25.930
All right.

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:26.680
Let's get started.

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:29.420
So like I said, today, I want to
focus in on Google Meeting Room

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:29.920
Solutions.

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:32.562
I want to talk about Google's
Hangouts Meet Hardware.

00:00:32.562 --> 00:00:35.020
Originally, we were going to
call this session, How G Suite

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:37.270
customers can use the new
Hangouts Meet hardware

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:39.840
kit to set up affordable,
reliable video conferencing

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:40.480
at scale.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:42.500
But apparently, that
was a little too wordy.

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:44.334
So we went with just
Hangouts Meet Hardware.

00:00:44.334 --> 00:00:45.916
But I thought it was
very descriptive.

00:00:45.916 --> 00:00:48.310
So that's really what we're
going to talk about today.

00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:49.930
You know, when it
comes to meetings,

00:00:49.930 --> 00:00:51.490
generally, people hate them.

00:00:51.490 --> 00:00:56.950
They, typically, to most people,
represent a productivity loss.

00:00:56.950 --> 00:00:59.350
More than half of
the people we polled

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:01.600
found that meetings were a
wasteful use of their time.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:03.310
And what that leads
to is most people

00:01:03.310 --> 00:01:05.260
start multitasking,
looking at their phone,

00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:07.520
maybe checking their email,
maybe not paying attention

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:08.740
to what's going on at all.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:10.759
And really, most people
feel that they're

00:01:10.759 --> 00:01:11.800
having too many meetings.

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:14.740
But the reality is, meetings
are actually increasing and not

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:15.550
decreasing.

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:17.380
Because at the end
of the day, they're

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:18.950
a fundamental part of business.

00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:22.030
They're incredibly important
to bringing new ideas

00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:26.560
to the table, creating
solutions to problems, and most

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:29.050
importantly, driving consensus
within your organization,

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:30.508
getting everybody
on the same page.

00:01:30.508 --> 00:01:32.620
So we felt we needed
to find a solution

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:35.170
to make these time
periods that much more

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:37.340
impactful for the organization.

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:39.490
Now, not only are we
getting more meetings that

00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:41.050
are getting less
productive, we're

00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:43.760
being asked to attend
them from more places.

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:45.447
More employees are
working remotely.

00:01:45.447 --> 00:01:47.530
But you still need to be
a part of those meetings,

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:49.600
no matter whether
you're on the other side

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:51.391
of the city, the other
side of the country,

00:01:51.391 --> 00:01:52.370
or around the world.

00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:56.920
And so Google looked into how
we could make meetings more

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:59.590
productive and more impactful.

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:01.420
So what we wanted
to do was first

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:04.600
increase the presence that you
could achieve in a meeting.

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:06.100
That would reduce
the multitasking

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:09.005
and really want to make it more
of a face-to-face experience.

00:02:09.005 --> 00:02:11.380
I like to think of there being
a social contract in place

00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:14.020
when you're face-to-face with
somebody that kind of demands

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:17.864
your attention and demands that
you be present in the moment.

00:02:17.864 --> 00:02:19.780
As well, on top of all
that, the body language

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:21.863
that you receive while
you're able to see somebody

00:02:21.863 --> 00:02:24.460
really increases your ability
to evaluate what they're saying,

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:27.610
what their mood is, and
really, what's behind the point

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:29.210
they're trying to get at.

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:31.960
We also spent a lot
of time analyzing

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:33.220
what makes teams successful.

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:36.280
Because, typically, meetings
are an important part

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:37.330
of any team engagement.

00:02:37.330 --> 00:02:39.835
And part of what we found
in our rework with Google--

00:02:39.835 --> 00:02:42.460
you can see there's a URL there,
if you want to find more about

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:44.290
these studies--

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:46.930
what we found was this concept
of psychological safety

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:51.100
was the number one factor
in making a team successful.

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:53.080
And that meant
that they had to be

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:56.260
not afraid to take risks, not
afraid to share their ideas.

00:02:56.260 --> 00:02:59.356
And that came with that
face-to-face interaction.

00:02:59.356 --> 00:03:00.730
That came with
knowing the people

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:02.230
you were working
with in a way that

00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:05.140
was a lot more intimate than
over a telephone conference

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:06.560
bridge.

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:08.600
So Google took all
these insights,

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:11.090
and we rolled out our own
video conferencing solution.

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:12.260
We did this years ago.

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:16.700
And in the evolution
of Google, as we

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:19.400
went from a very small
startup to a multinational

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:23.180
organization, we quickly
found that videoconferencing

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:25.250
was an essential part
of our growth strategy.

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:27.125
Not everybody wanted to
move to Mountain View

00:03:27.125 --> 00:03:30.410
and pay those real estate
prices and sit in the traffic.

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:33.270
So we found we had to have
employees around the world.

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:35.690
We also need to being agile
company that could adapt

00:03:35.690 --> 00:03:37.610
to market forces very quickly.

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:39.680
So we employed video
conferencing solutions

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:40.942
to do that.

00:03:40.942 --> 00:03:43.400
And we quickly found there were
some challenges to becoming

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:45.780
a video-centric organization.

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:47.450
First and foremost was scale--

00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:49.820
we needed a tool that could
handle Google's scale.

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:52.310
Google's scale was
100,000 employees

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:54.330
in 50 different countries.

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:56.750
We also needed tools
that were easy to use

00:03:56.750 --> 00:03:59.270
and would drive an engagement
level that we wanted.

00:03:59.270 --> 00:04:02.570
So we built out our own
video conferencing solution.

00:04:02.570 --> 00:04:05.060
It was made up of
20,000-- today, anyway--

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:08.570
20,000 videoconferencing-enabled
meeting rooms around the world

00:04:08.570 --> 00:04:13.400
that host over 275,000
meetings every day.

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:18.019
What that adds up to is 10 years
of videoconferencing meetings--

00:04:18.019 --> 00:04:19.220
or minutes, I should say--

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:19.929
every single day.

00:04:19.929 --> 00:04:21.220
Think about that for a minute--

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:24.050
10 years of video conferencing
that Google produces internally

00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:25.749
every single day.

00:04:25.749 --> 00:04:27.290
And like I said,
through this journey

00:04:27.290 --> 00:04:29.570
from being a
traditional organization

00:04:29.570 --> 00:04:31.790
to a video-centric
organization, we

00:04:31.790 --> 00:04:34.235
found there were a lot of
challenges and opportunities

00:04:34.235 --> 00:04:35.930
both in the
videoconferencing space.

00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:38.000
And so we needed to
build out a tool kit that

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:40.400
took advantage of those things
while it reduced the user

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:41.180
friction.

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:43.250
And so that's what these
four elements you see up

00:04:43.250 --> 00:04:46.370
on the screen here-- really, the
hardware and software elements

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:49.037
of our meeting room solution.

00:04:49.037 --> 00:04:51.120
At the core of everything
is Google Hangouts Meet.

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:53.220
It's a software
solution to provide

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:57.330
a high-quality videoconference
meeting from wherever you are.

00:04:57.330 --> 00:04:59.100
The hardware is for
your meeting rooms--

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:02.180
so a hardware kit that
creates enterprise-grade video

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:04.580
conferencing from
any meeting space.

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:07.014
The Jamboard brings
the whiteboard

00:05:07.014 --> 00:05:08.430
into to the video
conference world

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:10.846
and allows you to connect that
whiteboard into the meeting

00:05:10.846 --> 00:05:13.199
so you can share your ideas
and creations with anyone

00:05:13.199 --> 00:05:14.490
that's attached to the meeting.

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:16.590
And then the software
app allows you

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:19.740
to do that via any mobile device
so you can also participate

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:21.540
in that ideation session.

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:24.210
So it's these pieces that really
make up the Google meeting room

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:24.710
solution.

00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:27.630
Lets focus in a little bit,
first, on Google Hangouts Meet.

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:29.010
This is really
where it all began

00:05:29.010 --> 00:05:31.350
for Google videoconferencing.

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:34.410
We created our own tool,
delivered that over the web.

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:36.330
You just went to a
browser, typed in a URL,

00:05:36.330 --> 00:05:38.280
and you were part of a meeting.

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:42.280
And of course, we've since made
that available on mobile apps.

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:44.846
But really, the idea is to
make it super easy for anyone

00:05:44.846 --> 00:05:47.220
to schedule and join a meeting
without even really having

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:47.970
to think about it.

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:50.490
Because we don't want to set
any barriers to the adoption

00:05:50.490 --> 00:05:54.020
of that video-first culture.

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:56.180
Now, of course, there's
not always a time

00:05:56.180 --> 00:05:57.974
that you can get onto
a videoconference.

00:05:57.974 --> 00:05:59.390
Maybe you're
driving, and you just

00:05:59.390 --> 00:06:01.820
want to use the telephone in
its more traditional manner

00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:03.990
so that you can still
drive along safely.

00:06:03.990 --> 00:06:06.620
So we added a dial-in
to every single meeting.

00:06:06.620 --> 00:06:08.999
Not only does this make
it possible to choose

00:06:08.999 --> 00:06:10.790
how you want to join
the meeting and handle

00:06:10.790 --> 00:06:12.782
that on-the-go
scenario, it means

00:06:12.782 --> 00:06:14.990
that people, maybe, outside
of your organization that

00:06:14.990 --> 00:06:16.460
are a little bit more
familiar with dialing-in

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:17.880
can easily join that meeting.

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:20.570
It also means you can reduce
the cost of a telephone bridge

00:06:20.570 --> 00:06:23.670
that you need to support
within your organization.

00:06:23.670 --> 00:06:25.420
Now, of course, there
are also those times

00:06:25.420 --> 00:06:27.760
when you, unexpectedly,
need to dial out.

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:29.260
And that's another
piece we added.

00:06:29.260 --> 00:06:31.912
So maybe there's a participant
you want to add in last minute

00:06:31.912 --> 00:06:32.620
to your meeting--

00:06:32.620 --> 00:06:34.030
simply dial their phone number.

00:06:34.030 --> 00:06:36.310
And you can do that out
of any Meet to bring them

00:06:36.310 --> 00:06:38.440
into the conversation.

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:40.660
One of the things I notice
when I walk around Google

00:06:40.660 --> 00:06:42.790
is there isn't a
lot of desk phones.

00:06:42.790 --> 00:06:46.510
And really, what's allowed us
to get rid of the desk phone is

00:06:46.510 --> 00:06:49.690
this a capability for all of our
videoconference rooms to allow

00:06:49.690 --> 00:06:52.910
dial-in/dial-out capabilities.

00:06:52.910 --> 00:06:54.410
Now, the Meet
hardware, like I said,

00:06:54.410 --> 00:06:56.450
that's intended to
make the meeting

00:06:56.450 --> 00:06:59.240
space into an
enterprise-grade meeting room.

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:01.021
And really, it's a
fairly simple kit--

00:07:01.021 --> 00:07:02.270
you see it there on the left--

00:07:02.270 --> 00:07:05.510
but it transforms any space
into a video conference room.

00:07:05.510 --> 00:07:08.210
And again, a nuance that
I've noticed at Google

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:11.640
is every single space here
is a videoconference room.

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:13.910
You can go and see a
two-person huddle room

00:07:13.910 --> 00:07:17.120
that has a screen on the
wall, a camera, and a little

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:18.720
touchpad to join the meeting.

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:20.960
You can see large meeting
rooms of 20, 30 people that

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:23.150
are set up to host
large meetings that

00:07:23.150 --> 00:07:25.770
have that same kit installed
with, maybe a different camera,

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:28.370
but it allows that large
room to be part of it.

00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:31.400
So really the goal was to
create this hardware kit that

00:07:31.400 --> 00:07:34.230
fits any room but creates an
enterprise-grade experience

00:07:34.230 --> 00:07:36.560
that's easy to use.

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:39.220
Let's take a look at
it in more detail.

00:07:39.220 --> 00:07:42.570
So at the heart of the hardware
kit is the touch screen.

00:07:42.570 --> 00:07:45.420
That really allows you
to simply tap-to-go

00:07:45.420 --> 00:07:47.640
to join the meeting,
control the cameras

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:49.410
and every other
aspect of the meeting.

00:07:49.410 --> 00:07:51.534
Maybe you want to record
the meeting-- a simple tap

00:07:51.534 --> 00:07:54.810
on that same touchpad-- and
now that's being recorded right

00:07:54.810 --> 00:07:56.600
into your drive stream.

00:07:56.600 --> 00:07:58.620
The speakermic was
designed by Google

00:07:58.620 --> 00:08:00.620
to be a world-class
audio experience.

00:08:00.620 --> 00:08:03.870
Basically, we want to reduce
the noise and the echo

00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:07.290
that people were getting
over traditional speakermics,

00:08:07.290 --> 00:08:11.160
so we've added that into the
mix and provided the latest

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:13.120
equipment and software
for making that

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:16.407
a crisp, echo-free experience.

00:08:16.407 --> 00:08:18.240
The camera options--
there are actually two.

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:21.940
And like I said, there's a large
and a sort of small or regular

00:08:21.940 --> 00:08:24.250
boardroom flavor.

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:26.620
For smaller rooms,
the Huddly camera

00:08:26.620 --> 00:08:29.860
has onboard machine learning
that will crop and zoom

00:08:29.860 --> 00:08:32.650
based on recognizing
faces in the meeting room.

00:08:32.650 --> 00:08:35.470
So this will allow you
to maximize the image

00:08:35.470 --> 00:08:38.169
and make for a great
user experience

00:08:38.169 --> 00:08:41.480
without the users having
to do any zoom or pan.

00:08:41.480 --> 00:08:43.312
It also has a nice
wide field of view

00:08:43.312 --> 00:08:44.770
so it captures
people on the edges.

00:08:44.770 --> 00:08:47.770
If you think of your traditional
four-person or six-person

00:08:47.770 --> 00:08:50.680
rooms, sometimes they're
spread wide instead of long.

00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:53.062
This wide field of view
captures the entire audience.

00:08:53.062 --> 00:08:55.270
On the larger boardroom
size-- that's where you have,

00:08:55.270 --> 00:08:57.830
traditionally, long
meeting rooms--

00:08:57.830 --> 00:09:02.920
our Logitech PTZ 2 allows you a
10x optical zoom, if you need,

00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:04.670
and the controls built
into that touchpad,

00:09:04.670 --> 00:09:08.560
like I said, to control the
camera all from one place.

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:11.860
The brains of the meeting
room kit is the Chromebox.

00:09:11.860 --> 00:09:14.140
We keep building these
faster and stronger and more

00:09:14.140 --> 00:09:17.110
powerful to try and
optimize the quality

00:09:17.110 --> 00:09:18.640
of the entire experience.

00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:20.950
But more importantly,
these devices all

00:09:20.950 --> 00:09:23.080
connect into that
Chromebox, and it handles

00:09:23.080 --> 00:09:24.950
all the over-the-air updates.

00:09:24.950 --> 00:09:28.390
So when we come out with a new
echo-cancellation algorithm

00:09:28.390 --> 00:09:31.630
for that speakermic, it's passed
down through the Chromebox

00:09:31.630 --> 00:09:33.490
to the speakermic
connected to it.

00:09:33.490 --> 00:09:35.823
And you don't have to take
care of managing any of that.

00:09:35.823 --> 00:09:39.240
It just happens
automatigically over the air.

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:41.070
Now, when you think
of Google, I hope

00:09:41.070 --> 00:09:43.500
you think of the world's
easiest to use website--

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:45.171
one text box and one button.

00:09:45.171 --> 00:09:46.920
And that's a design
philosophy that Google

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:49.290
tries to really bring to
anything that we build.

00:09:49.290 --> 00:09:51.330
And I think we've done
an amazing job here

00:09:51.330 --> 00:09:55.790
by keeping the idea of Google
meeting room solution simple.

00:09:55.790 --> 00:09:57.630
It's first, simple
on your budget.

00:09:57.630 --> 00:10:00.810
The goal in making
these kits was

00:10:00.810 --> 00:10:02.400
to make a kit that
fit every space--

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:05.130
and I mean to put in every
space so that you don't

00:10:05.130 --> 00:10:06.507
leave any space behind, right?

00:10:06.507 --> 00:10:09.090
You don't want people having to
make decisions of whether they

00:10:09.090 --> 00:10:10.340
have a videoconference or not.

00:10:10.340 --> 00:10:12.900
Every room needs to be enabled,
so when they have a meeting,

00:10:12.900 --> 00:10:14.775
the videoconference is
almost an afterthought

00:10:14.775 --> 00:10:16.720
because it just happens.

00:10:16.720 --> 00:10:18.270
But in order to
do that, we needed

00:10:18.270 --> 00:10:20.820
a kit that was
affordable on your budget

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:24.350
and would fit into
that overall AV spend.

00:10:24.350 --> 00:10:27.090
It needed to be easy
for your end users

00:10:27.090 --> 00:10:30.690
to, A, set up a meeting, and
then, B, join that meeting

00:10:30.690 --> 00:10:31.794
when they need to.

00:10:31.794 --> 00:10:33.210
And that makes it,
in turn, easier

00:10:33.210 --> 00:10:35.168
for you to support because
you don't have users

00:10:35.168 --> 00:10:37.100
having to figure
things out or trying

00:10:37.100 --> 00:10:40.980
to fix equipment in the room
that they should not be fixing.

00:10:40.980 --> 00:10:44.550
So we made it very easy
for the end users--

00:10:44.550 --> 00:10:46.040
built right into
Google Calendar,

00:10:46.040 --> 00:10:47.340
if you use Google Calendar.

00:10:47.340 --> 00:10:49.080
You don't have to do anything
other than invite people

00:10:49.080 --> 00:10:49.740
to the meeting.

00:10:49.740 --> 00:10:53.100
The meeting connection
URL is automatically

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:54.330
added to that invite.

00:10:54.330 --> 00:10:57.000
When you invite a room,
that meeting information

00:10:57.000 --> 00:10:58.950
is sent right to the
room where it appears

00:10:58.950 --> 00:11:00.745
on the Memo touchpad to use.

00:11:00.745 --> 00:11:02.370
So it's very easy
for your users, which

00:11:02.370 --> 00:11:05.880
means less support you're going
to have to do as the AV team

00:11:05.880 --> 00:11:07.460
supporting that.

00:11:07.460 --> 00:11:10.830
And lastly but not least, the
ease of install, deployment,

00:11:10.830 --> 00:11:13.140
and management-- so the
actual heavy lifting that

00:11:13.140 --> 00:11:16.200
needs to go on behind-the-scenes
to make this experience-- we've

00:11:16.200 --> 00:11:17.760
made that as easy as possible.

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:19.560
Fairly plug-and-play
on the installation.

00:11:19.560 --> 00:11:24.510
You connect the pieces
together, a little bit of log-in

00:11:24.510 --> 00:11:29.130
work just to get the hardware
initialized, and away you go.

00:11:29.130 --> 00:11:31.380
From there, it's all managed
within the admin console.

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:32.760
Over-the-air
updates make it easy

00:11:32.760 --> 00:11:36.520
for me to not have to
think about managing that.

00:11:36.520 --> 00:11:39.607
And like I said,
the admin console

00:11:39.607 --> 00:11:41.940
that you use to manage G
Suite-- it's the same place you

00:11:41.940 --> 00:11:43.555
go to manage these rooms.

00:11:43.555 --> 00:11:45.430
But not only that-- you
can manage its scale.

00:11:45.430 --> 00:11:48.700
You can see where all your rooms
are at, what state they're at.

00:11:48.700 --> 00:11:54.810
We've continued adding more
reporting and analytics

00:11:54.810 --> 00:11:56.940
capabilities as well
as notifications

00:11:56.940 --> 00:11:59.070
to let you know,
proactively, when something

00:11:59.070 --> 00:12:00.997
is going wrong with the room.

00:12:00.997 --> 00:12:03.330
We're also looking at ways
to help you improve your ROI.

00:12:03.330 --> 00:12:05.040
And there's a twofold
strategy here.

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:07.020
One is that really
low price point

00:12:07.020 --> 00:12:08.850
of these kits to
make sure that you're

00:12:08.850 --> 00:12:12.420
getting an ROI on your AV
budget when you build out

00:12:12.420 --> 00:12:13.290
every single space.

00:12:13.290 --> 00:12:16.560
But also, we're providing
metrics on room utilization,

00:12:16.560 --> 00:12:18.435
so you can take advantage
of that real estate

00:12:18.435 --> 00:12:20.970
that you've paid for and make
sure it's being used properly

00:12:20.970 --> 00:12:22.340
and not being left behind.

00:12:22.340 --> 00:12:25.790
A lot of times when, for
example, videoconferencing

00:12:25.790 --> 00:12:27.900
equipment goes bust, that
room just doesn't get

00:12:27.900 --> 00:12:28.960
used for weeks and weeks.

00:12:28.960 --> 00:12:31.320
But you're still paying for
that in terms of the real estate

00:12:31.320 --> 00:12:32.278
costs and the overhead.

00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:36.830
That ease-of-use, that one tap--

00:12:36.830 --> 00:12:39.530
I think that's really one
of the biggest things I

00:12:39.530 --> 00:12:41.042
noticed when I came to Google.

00:12:41.042 --> 00:12:42.500
When I walked into
a meeting, all I

00:12:42.500 --> 00:12:46.340
had to do to join that
meeting was tap on a touchpad.

00:12:46.340 --> 00:12:48.080
And that's what we've
brought to market

00:12:48.080 --> 00:12:51.192
with this latest version of
our Hangouts Meet hardware kit.

00:12:51.192 --> 00:12:53.150
As I said before, when
you calendar your event,

00:12:53.150 --> 00:12:55.040
that's really where the
magic starts to happen.

00:12:55.040 --> 00:12:56.360
Google will help
me along the way

00:12:56.360 --> 00:12:57.860
to figure out the right
room and the people

00:12:57.860 --> 00:12:59.109
I need to invite and all that.

00:12:59.109 --> 00:13:03.080
But it will also place that
meeting URL in the calendar

00:13:03.080 --> 00:13:05.790
and send it to the room itself,
so I don't have to do anything.

00:13:05.790 --> 00:13:07.550
I just go into the
room, tap, and go,

00:13:07.550 --> 00:13:09.675
and my meeting starts on
the screen in front of me.

00:13:12.770 --> 00:13:14.312
I mentioned the
over-the-air updates.

00:13:14.312 --> 00:13:16.645
That means I don't have to
walk around from room to room

00:13:16.645 --> 00:13:17.720
to support this hardware.

00:13:17.720 --> 00:13:19.940
It simply happens when
we push out a new update

00:13:19.940 --> 00:13:22.820
to any of the attached
qualified peripherals.

00:13:22.820 --> 00:13:27.330
They'll receive that update
and implement that right away.

00:13:27.330 --> 00:13:29.910
There's self-monitoring and
self-remediating capabilities,

00:13:29.910 --> 00:13:32.045
so the system will try
and correct itself.

00:13:32.045 --> 00:13:35.400
And you don't have to actually
go in and do a restart

00:13:35.400 --> 00:13:37.400
or refresh the system
when something goes wrong.

00:13:37.400 --> 00:13:42.230
It will try those basic health
fix options before it actually

00:13:42.230 --> 00:13:44.800
notifies you.

00:13:44.800 --> 00:13:47.620
And then, lastly, the automatic
peripheral firmware updates

00:13:47.620 --> 00:13:50.800
that go right to that
Chromebox itself.

00:13:50.800 --> 00:13:53.750
Now, all of this is actually
also leveraging something

00:13:53.750 --> 00:13:57.290
that Google is very known
for in the YouTube space--

00:13:57.290 --> 00:13:59.450
this global
infrastructure-- our ability

00:13:59.450 --> 00:14:02.119
to pass video around the
world and make it available

00:14:02.119 --> 00:14:03.410
no matter where you're located.

00:14:03.410 --> 00:14:05.480
We leverage that
same infrastructure

00:14:05.480 --> 00:14:07.400
of data centers and networks.

00:14:07.400 --> 00:14:09.650
Google runs one of
the largest, if not

00:14:09.650 --> 00:14:12.170
the largest software-defined
network in the world,

00:14:12.170 --> 00:14:16.630
running our own pipes under
the Atlantic and Pacific Ocean.

00:14:16.630 --> 00:14:19.910
My point being here, you're
getting the same ability

00:14:19.910 --> 00:14:22.460
to move video around the world
that we bring to YouTube.

00:14:26.060 --> 00:14:29.660
Now, one of the best
aspects of Google Hangouts

00:14:29.660 --> 00:14:31.367
is the way it's
integrated into G-suite.

00:14:31.367 --> 00:14:32.950
And that means a few
different things.

00:14:32.950 --> 00:14:34.825
First, there's that easy
Calendar integration

00:14:34.825 --> 00:14:36.140
I've mentioned a few times.

00:14:36.140 --> 00:14:37.940
Once your room is
a resource, it's

00:14:37.940 --> 00:14:39.200
going to show up in Calendar.

00:14:39.200 --> 00:14:40.910
It's going to
automatically be suggested

00:14:40.910 --> 00:14:42.680
when it's nearby
the user, and when

00:14:42.680 --> 00:14:44.120
it fits the amount of people
that have been invited

00:14:44.120 --> 00:14:45.260
to the meeting,
which is just going

00:14:45.260 --> 00:14:47.940
to make that whole experience
of setting up the meeting very,

00:14:47.940 --> 00:14:49.280
very easy.

00:14:49.280 --> 00:14:51.770
The Jamboard is
also connected in.

00:14:51.770 --> 00:14:55.490
So maybe I've paired my meeting
room hardware with my Jamboard.

00:14:55.490 --> 00:14:57.050
It will ask to join the meeting.

00:14:57.050 --> 00:14:58.880
Or I can just simply
type in the code,

00:14:58.880 --> 00:15:01.750
and now make Jamboard is a
participant in that meeting.

00:15:01.750 --> 00:15:03.167
Google Drive is
connected in here.

00:15:03.167 --> 00:15:05.500
So when I do a recording,
it's going to automatically go

00:15:05.500 --> 00:15:06.370
into Google Drive.

00:15:06.370 --> 00:15:07.750
I get the same
ease of sharing it

00:15:07.750 --> 00:15:10.750
with others that I do with
any file type in Google Drive.

00:15:10.750 --> 00:15:12.250
And really, we've
made that super

00:15:12.250 --> 00:15:14.080
easy from the
end-user perspective

00:15:14.080 --> 00:15:17.169
of just tapping in and
recording on that touch panel.

00:15:17.169 --> 00:15:19.210
And lastly, I've mentioned
a few times, the Admin

00:15:19.210 --> 00:15:20.714
Console for ease of manage.

00:15:20.714 --> 00:15:22.630
The other thing about
the G Suite integration,

00:15:22.630 --> 00:15:24.810
though, is a Google
Hangouts Meet

00:15:24.810 --> 00:15:26.750
is a part of that platform.

00:15:26.750 --> 00:15:29.710
So right away, you're getting a
free videoconferencing solution

00:15:29.710 --> 00:15:32.180
on everybody's laptop,
everybody's telephone

00:15:32.180 --> 00:15:34.390
that they can begin
using right away.

00:15:34.390 --> 00:15:36.460
So that's included in
that G Suite bundle--

00:15:36.460 --> 00:15:39.160
that ability to videoconference,
to schedule meetings,

00:15:39.160 --> 00:15:41.567
and really, have a
videoconference in my pocket

00:15:41.567 --> 00:15:42.150
wherever I go.

00:15:45.012 --> 00:15:47.220
I've mentioned a few times
this Calendar integration.

00:15:47.220 --> 00:15:50.445
But I do think that's a really
key part of the experience,

00:15:50.445 --> 00:15:52.456
so I want to really
highlight that.

00:15:52.456 --> 00:15:54.330
You can see I've got a
little animation here.

00:15:54.330 --> 00:15:56.300
As I'm adding
guests, the Calendar

00:15:56.300 --> 00:15:59.200
is going to suggest guests--
not based on alphabetical order,

00:15:59.200 --> 00:16:01.230
but based on a
machine-learning algorithm that

00:16:01.230 --> 00:16:03.180
knows I work with Katie a lot.

00:16:03.180 --> 00:16:05.069
So I'll put her at
the top because that's

00:16:05.069 --> 00:16:06.360
who I'm typically meeting with.

00:16:06.360 --> 00:16:09.480
Or these are some people I've
had some recent interactions

00:16:09.480 --> 00:16:11.550
with, so let's pop
them into the list

00:16:11.550 --> 00:16:13.262
as well because
there's obviously a lot

00:16:13.262 --> 00:16:14.470
going on with them right now.

00:16:14.470 --> 00:16:16.520
And as I type, it's going
to get even smarter.

00:16:16.520 --> 00:16:19.410
So after I put in
an S, we'll just

00:16:19.410 --> 00:16:24.610
have Scott and Srivatsan
on that list there as well.

00:16:24.610 --> 00:16:27.865
And then, of course, when
it gets to finding a time--

00:16:27.865 --> 00:16:29.490
once I've got my
guests-- maybe they're

00:16:29.490 --> 00:16:30.960
not all available at that time.

00:16:30.960 --> 00:16:32.130
I can look for times.

00:16:32.130 --> 00:16:34.620
But the system will also
suggest times for me

00:16:34.620 --> 00:16:37.920
and make it easy for me to find
a time where we can all meet.

00:16:37.920 --> 00:16:39.930
Adding rooms-- it
will suggest rooms

00:16:39.930 --> 00:16:42.150
based on the locations
of the participants.

00:16:42.150 --> 00:16:44.880
So when I'm scheduling a
meeting for people in New York,

00:16:44.880 --> 00:16:46.590
it's going to suggest
New York rooms.

00:16:46.590 --> 00:16:48.631
But if I have a few people
out on the West Coast,

00:16:48.631 --> 00:16:51.420
it'll give me two sets of
suggestions-- one for New York

00:16:51.420 --> 00:16:54.520
and maybe one for Mountain View.

00:16:54.520 --> 00:16:56.290
In the room, that
Calendar invite

00:16:56.290 --> 00:16:58.080
just gets passed
right to the touchpad.

00:16:58.080 --> 00:16:59.740
And so I'll see
all the events that

00:16:59.740 --> 00:17:01.990
are scheduled for that day.

00:17:01.990 --> 00:17:03.490
And I can simply
tap on my meeting

00:17:03.490 --> 00:17:05.560
to join that whenever
that comes up.

00:17:05.560 --> 00:17:09.010
And that touchpad has
full control capabilities

00:17:09.010 --> 00:17:11.980
of both the camera, my
ability to record the meeting.

00:17:11.980 --> 00:17:15.940
It'll also show me the
in-Calendar context

00:17:15.940 --> 00:17:16.670
of the meeting.

00:17:16.670 --> 00:17:18.670
So a lot of times, you
get going back to back

00:17:18.670 --> 00:17:19.750
from meeting to meeting.

00:17:19.750 --> 00:17:21.970
You're not even sure
what you're walking into.

00:17:21.970 --> 00:17:24.720
You don't have time to pull it
up on your phone or your laptop

00:17:24.720 --> 00:17:27.934
maybe, but you can just tap
a button on the touchpad

00:17:27.934 --> 00:17:29.350
and see the details
of the meeting

00:17:29.350 --> 00:17:31.690
and just get yourself
a few more seconds

00:17:31.690 --> 00:17:35.819
of being caught up
before you jump in.

00:17:35.819 --> 00:17:37.360
I mentioned the
Jamboard a few times.

00:17:37.360 --> 00:17:39.850
And there's probably
a whole hour

00:17:39.850 --> 00:17:41.860
we could put on the
Jamboard conversation.

00:17:41.860 --> 00:17:44.590
But the idea here was
to bring the whiteboard

00:17:44.590 --> 00:17:45.964
into the video conference world.

00:17:45.964 --> 00:17:48.130
Probably a lot of you have
been on video conferences

00:17:48.130 --> 00:17:51.190
where somebody at the other
end of the videoconference

00:17:51.190 --> 00:17:53.170
has had a whiteboard.

00:17:53.170 --> 00:17:56.321
You get this poor
experience and don't

00:17:56.321 --> 00:17:57.820
get to actually
share in the ability

00:17:57.820 --> 00:17:59.654
to create ideas or
correct problems

00:17:59.654 --> 00:18:00.820
that are on that whiteboard.

00:18:00.820 --> 00:18:03.195
Maybe you don't even get to
see the whiteboard very well,

00:18:03.195 --> 00:18:06.280
because it's off to
an angle or not that

00:18:06.280 --> 00:18:08.090
easy to see through the camera.

00:18:08.090 --> 00:18:09.780
And if you think
about the whiteboard,

00:18:09.780 --> 00:18:13.300
it's been around since people
were drawing in the sand.

00:18:13.300 --> 00:18:16.090
It's that open space that you
create ideas and solve problems

00:18:16.090 --> 00:18:18.340
or huddle around a
last-minute problem that

00:18:18.340 --> 00:18:22.880
needs to be handled in a
new and unexpected way.

00:18:22.880 --> 00:18:25.420
And so losing that tool in
a videoconference world,

00:18:25.420 --> 00:18:27.940
we found that created a
lot of friction for Google.

00:18:27.940 --> 00:18:30.300
We invented the Jamboard
to solve for that.

00:18:30.300 --> 00:18:32.620
It allows you to connect
the boards to each other,

00:18:32.620 --> 00:18:35.110
individually, or
connect into the meeting

00:18:35.110 --> 00:18:38.440
and share that whiteboard space
with any participant that's

00:18:38.440 --> 00:18:40.127
on there.

00:18:40.127 --> 00:18:42.460
And as I mentioned, all of
this is seamlessly integrated

00:18:42.460 --> 00:18:43.540
into G Suite.

00:18:43.540 --> 00:18:45.790
When you deploy G Suite,
you get all the benefits

00:18:45.790 --> 00:18:47.950
of Google Hangout Meet.

00:18:47.950 --> 00:18:49.600
And when you add in
the meeting rooms,

00:18:49.600 --> 00:18:51.225
they just become a
part of the Calendar

00:18:51.225 --> 00:18:54.920
and a part of the
user experience.

00:18:54.920 --> 00:18:58.750
Now, more recently, we've added
even more power to the Google

00:18:58.750 --> 00:19:01.000
Hangouts Meet
capabilities, and that's

00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:04.330
through a partnership with
our friends over at Pexip.

00:19:04.330 --> 00:19:07.420
So we've created a
native interop solution

00:19:07.420 --> 00:19:11.230
that allows anyone to
join a Google Hangout Meet

00:19:11.230 --> 00:19:16.030
from a SIP H.323 endpoint, such
as Cisco, Polycom, Lifesize,

00:19:16.030 --> 00:19:17.362
or from Skype for Business.

00:19:17.362 --> 00:19:18.820
So really, what
this means is, when

00:19:18.820 --> 00:19:21.490
you schedule an event,
the same way it easily

00:19:21.490 --> 00:19:24.340
creates a link for
you to join a Hangout,

00:19:24.340 --> 00:19:28.540
you now gain a link to join
from those other endpoints.

00:19:28.540 --> 00:19:32.530
So I have the ability to
go onto a Cisco endpoint,

00:19:32.530 --> 00:19:34.840
punch in my code, and
join in that Hangout Meet.

00:19:34.840 --> 00:19:37.840
That's bringing that legacy
investment that you maybe

00:19:37.840 --> 00:19:39.820
have in some of
your meeting rooms

00:19:39.820 --> 00:19:42.790
and bringing that into the
Google Hangout Meet world

00:19:42.790 --> 00:19:43.452
as well.

00:19:43.452 --> 00:19:45.910
So if I have some spaces that
I've already heavily invested

00:19:45.910 --> 00:19:48.970
in, I can now take
advantage of that investment

00:19:48.970 --> 00:19:53.020
while still rolling out Google
Hangouts Meet hardware in some

00:19:53.020 --> 00:19:55.860
of my smaller, less-used rooms.

00:19:55.860 --> 00:19:57.480
It also means that,
if I'm working

00:19:57.480 --> 00:20:00.000
with people that maybe aren't
on Google Hangouts Meet--

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:02.270
maybe they're on one of
these other systems--

00:20:02.270 --> 00:20:04.020
I'm able to send them
an invite, and we're

00:20:04.020 --> 00:20:06.150
able to collaborate
together online as well.

00:20:09.070 --> 00:20:11.940
You can see here just
kind of a glamour

00:20:11.940 --> 00:20:15.300
shot of all the companies
we work with on the Google

00:20:15.300 --> 00:20:17.880
Hangouts Meet side.

00:20:17.880 --> 00:20:19.380
I think the real
thick takeaway here

00:20:19.380 --> 00:20:22.870
is that this is a solution that
fits customers of all sizes.

00:20:22.870 --> 00:20:25.890
So whether it's a small company
that traditionally wouldn't

00:20:25.890 --> 00:20:27.600
be able to afford
videoconferencing

00:20:27.600 --> 00:20:30.100
or large organizations that
are really looking to increase

00:20:30.100 --> 00:20:32.520
their collaboration and
transform their culture,

00:20:32.520 --> 00:20:35.570
every organization can take
advantage of the Google

00:20:35.570 --> 00:20:39.700
Hangouts Meet solution.

00:20:39.700 --> 00:20:42.710
If you have any questions
on Google Hangouts Meet,

00:20:42.710 --> 00:20:47.570
we've created this alias here--
meeting-room-pod@google.com.

00:20:47.570 --> 00:20:49.370
So that's myself and my team.

00:20:49.370 --> 00:20:52.730
We're standing by to
answer follow-up questions

00:20:52.730 --> 00:20:55.225
or things that maybe just
go beyond the session today.

00:20:55.225 --> 00:20:57.350
But definitely, if you'd
like to set up a follow-up

00:20:57.350 --> 00:21:00.260
conversation, feel free
to reach out to this email

00:21:00.260 --> 00:21:03.110
and get a hold of us.

00:21:03.110 --> 00:21:05.712
So that's it for the
lecture portion of this.

00:21:05.712 --> 00:21:06.670
Stay tuned for a live--

00:22:45.680 --> 00:22:46.180
All right.

00:22:46.180 --> 00:22:46.990
Welcome back.

00:22:46.990 --> 00:22:49.390
Now I'd like to take a few
questions from the audience.

00:22:49.390 --> 00:22:51.070
So we've put a few up here.

00:22:51.070 --> 00:22:53.260
By all means, feel free
to keep asking questions.

00:22:53.260 --> 00:22:56.140
We have people online waiting
to help you with those.

00:22:56.140 --> 00:22:57.800
Let's start off
with the first one.

00:22:57.800 --> 00:23:00.700
"What is the cost for the
meeting hardware kit?"

00:23:00.700 --> 00:23:04.390
OK, so we have two flavors
of meeting hardware kit.

00:23:04.390 --> 00:23:08.650
There's the regular size room--
and I think this is anything 10

00:23:08.650 --> 00:23:10.180
people or less--

00:23:10.180 --> 00:23:12.950
as well as our large
room kit, as well.

00:23:12.950 --> 00:23:16.300
It's approximately $2000
for the regular size kit,

00:23:16.300 --> 00:23:19.600
and that comes with a camera,
a speakermic, the Chromebox,

00:23:19.600 --> 00:23:20.560
and the touchpad.

00:23:20.560 --> 00:23:21.790
You bring your own monitor.

00:23:21.790 --> 00:23:25.184
We didn't want to force people
into a particular monitor,

00:23:25.184 --> 00:23:27.100
since different rooms
require different sizes.

00:23:27.100 --> 00:23:29.980
And you may already have an
existing monitor investment

00:23:29.980 --> 00:23:32.470
or screen investment that you're
able to take advantage of.

00:23:32.470 --> 00:23:36.130
The larger kit, which comes
with the touchpad, Chromebox,

00:23:36.130 --> 00:23:41.560
speakermic, and a Logitech PTZ 2
camera is approximately $2,500.

00:23:41.560 --> 00:23:46.650
And you can find more
about that online as well.

00:23:46.650 --> 00:23:48.750
"Why is the new
Hangouts Meet hardware

00:23:48.750 --> 00:23:52.940
better or different than
old Chromebox for Meetings?"

00:23:52.940 --> 00:23:54.990
So there's a bit to this.

00:23:54.990 --> 00:23:58.180
There's the software side
and the hardware side.

00:23:58.180 --> 00:24:01.380
So let's talk just the
hardware side for now.

00:24:01.380 --> 00:24:05.880
When we first came out with
the Chromebox for Meetings,

00:24:05.880 --> 00:24:09.660
the idea was to create a
disruptive videoconferencing

00:24:09.660 --> 00:24:10.680
kit.

00:24:10.680 --> 00:24:13.170
We wanted to find
a price point that

00:24:13.170 --> 00:24:15.540
meant every meeting
space could have

00:24:15.540 --> 00:24:17.670
videoconference inside of it.

00:24:17.670 --> 00:24:21.750
And our first foray was to,
for all intents and purposes,

00:24:21.750 --> 00:24:27.130
really build a kit from
consumer-available peripherals.

00:24:27.130 --> 00:24:29.179
So the camera that
came with it, you

00:24:29.179 --> 00:24:30.470
could probably buy at Best Buy.

00:24:30.470 --> 00:24:35.395
The speakermic was a fairly
basic puck configuration.

00:24:35.395 --> 00:24:36.780
It, of course,
had the Chromebox,

00:24:36.780 --> 00:24:41.010
but it leveraged a remote
control instead of the touch

00:24:41.010 --> 00:24:41.724
screen.

00:24:41.724 --> 00:24:44.140
And that provided the disruption
that we were looking for.

00:24:44.140 --> 00:24:48.810
It definitely allowed
companies to place these kits

00:24:48.810 --> 00:24:49.680
in more spaces.

00:24:49.680 --> 00:24:52.710
It was almost half the cost
of our existing hardware kit

00:24:52.710 --> 00:24:53.580
today.

00:24:53.580 --> 00:24:57.180
But what we also found was it
wasn't the enterprise grade

00:24:57.180 --> 00:25:00.405
that our customers were
expecting from the G Suite

00:25:00.405 --> 00:25:01.729
and Google products.

00:25:01.729 --> 00:25:03.270
So we went back to
the drawing board.

00:25:03.270 --> 00:25:04.610
We did do a lot on
the software side.

00:25:04.610 --> 00:25:06.276
I'll talk about that
a little bit later.

00:25:06.276 --> 00:25:09.130
But on the hardware side, we
re-engineered every aspect

00:25:09.130 --> 00:25:09.630
of it.

00:25:09.630 --> 00:25:11.477
So we introduced
the Memo touch pad

00:25:11.477 --> 00:25:12.810
instead of using the controller.

00:25:12.810 --> 00:25:14.770
That created a much
better user experience.

00:25:14.770 --> 00:25:17.550
So instead of navigating up and
down and having to use a small

00:25:17.550 --> 00:25:19.710
controller-- if you're like
me and sometimes forget

00:25:19.710 --> 00:25:22.650
your glasses, that can be
almost an impossible task--

00:25:22.650 --> 00:25:25.110
but it was replaced
by the simple touch,

00:25:25.110 --> 00:25:28.140
tap-and-go functionality
of the touch screen.

00:25:28.140 --> 00:25:30.030
We took the speakermic
and re-engineered

00:25:30.030 --> 00:25:32.880
that from the ground up,
really focusing in on noise

00:25:32.880 --> 00:25:34.380
and echo cancellation.

00:25:34.380 --> 00:25:37.110
So you get a
higher-quality audio,

00:25:37.110 --> 00:25:38.610
but you also get
less interference

00:25:38.610 --> 00:25:40.080
and less distraction.

00:25:40.080 --> 00:25:42.570
We also re-engineered
the Chromebox.

00:25:42.570 --> 00:25:46.330
We continually improve that
and provide better specs

00:25:46.330 --> 00:25:48.600
and hardware pieces.

00:25:48.600 --> 00:25:51.750
And then lastly, we introduced
two new camera options--

00:25:51.750 --> 00:25:55.680
one was the Logitech
PTZ 2, as well as

00:25:55.680 --> 00:25:59.010
the Huddly camera, which has
onboard machine learning that

00:25:59.010 --> 00:26:02.070
supports the auto crop-and-zoom
that I mentioned earlier

00:26:02.070 --> 00:26:06.490
to make the maximum impact
for different users.

00:26:09.232 --> 00:26:10.050
Let's see here.

00:26:10.050 --> 00:26:13.200
"What's better about Meet
hardware versus joining calls

00:26:13.200 --> 00:26:14.550
from my laptop?"

00:26:14.550 --> 00:26:18.075
I think, really, it's about
what your use case is.

00:26:18.075 --> 00:26:20.042
So the Meet hardware
was intended

00:26:20.042 --> 00:26:21.375
to go in the meeting room space.

00:26:21.375 --> 00:26:24.100
There's a lot of times when you
just need to meet as a group.

00:26:24.100 --> 00:26:26.130
And by no means do I
think videoconference

00:26:26.130 --> 00:26:30.310
has gotten to the point where it
outdoes the in-person meeting.

00:26:30.310 --> 00:26:32.477
So you're still going have
people meeting in person.

00:26:32.477 --> 00:26:34.976
But a lot of times, you're going
to have a mixed group where

00:26:34.976 --> 00:26:37.000
maybe five of you are
in the same location,

00:26:37.000 --> 00:26:40.840
but another five, or even
one person, is remote.

00:26:40.840 --> 00:26:42.940
That remote person can
join from their laptop,

00:26:42.940 --> 00:26:44.800
whereas the five of
you can be in the room.

00:26:44.800 --> 00:26:46.630
If you've ever been
in one meeting room

00:26:46.630 --> 00:26:49.240
where five people have tried
to join from the same laptop,

00:26:49.240 --> 00:26:51.500
you notice it's
fraught with problems.

00:26:51.500 --> 00:26:54.550
First of all, there is
the inevitable noise

00:26:54.550 --> 00:26:58.330
that's created by the feedback
loop as everybody is joining

00:26:58.330 --> 00:27:01.780
and their audio goes
into the speakermic.

00:27:01.780 --> 00:27:05.320
You can reduce that by just
using one person's laptop.

00:27:05.320 --> 00:27:06.820
But now everybody's
huddling around.

00:27:06.820 --> 00:27:09.380
And really, you're only
seeing one person on that.

00:27:09.380 --> 00:27:11.500
So the goal behind
the Meet hardware kit

00:27:11.500 --> 00:27:13.600
was to videoconference
enable your room

00:27:13.600 --> 00:27:17.330
and really make it so that I'm
meeting as if I'm face to face,

00:27:17.330 --> 00:27:19.240
but I'm using the
videoconferencing solution

00:27:19.240 --> 00:27:19.930
to do that.

00:27:19.930 --> 00:27:22.960
The laptop is more for that
one person that's remote

00:27:22.960 --> 00:27:24.880
or where I have to
join where I don't

00:27:24.880 --> 00:27:27.027
have a meeting room available.

00:27:27.027 --> 00:27:28.860
So really, I guess, to
answer that question,

00:27:28.860 --> 00:27:31.080
it's really about the
space you're using.

00:27:31.080 --> 00:27:33.330
And we would still
highly recommend

00:27:33.330 --> 00:27:37.290
outfitting your meeting
rooms with the hardware kit.

00:27:37.290 --> 00:27:39.750
We have a lot of investment
in our existing meeting rooms

00:27:39.750 --> 00:27:42.030
with Cisco, Polycom, et cetera.

00:27:42.030 --> 00:27:43.650
Is Hangouts Meet compatible?

00:27:43.650 --> 00:27:45.190
And in short, I'd
say, yes, it is.

00:27:45.190 --> 00:27:47.711
I just talked about
that Pexip partnership.

00:27:47.711 --> 00:27:49.710
You can definitely find
out more by reaching out

00:27:49.710 --> 00:27:52.524
to us or our friends at Pexip.

00:27:52.524 --> 00:27:54.690
They're very excited about
this partnership as well.

00:27:54.690 --> 00:27:56.790
What it really boils
down to is, when

00:27:56.790 --> 00:28:00.750
you create that Calendar invite,
in addition to scheduling

00:28:00.750 --> 00:28:02.640
the Hangouts Meet,
it will also provide

00:28:02.640 --> 00:28:07.050
options for how to join
via the Cisco and Polycom,

00:28:07.050 --> 00:28:12.240
and other SIP H.323 endpoints
as well as Skype for Business.

00:28:12.240 --> 00:28:14.310
As I mentioned, this is
a partnership with Pexip.

00:28:14.310 --> 00:28:16.220
So I would definitely
want to talk to you

00:28:16.220 --> 00:28:18.303
about how you make that
available for your company

00:28:18.303 --> 00:28:21.930
and what's involved in
setting up this integration.

00:28:21.930 --> 00:28:24.510
"Hangouts Meet versus
Hangouts Classic--"

00:28:24.510 --> 00:28:26.514
so this is really that
software question.

00:28:26.514 --> 00:28:27.930
Before I talked
about the hardware

00:28:27.930 --> 00:28:31.301
side of the differences
between the new Hangouts Meet

00:28:31.301 --> 00:28:33.300
hardware versus the old
Chromebox for Meetings--

00:28:33.300 --> 00:28:35.330
now let's talk about
the software side.

00:28:35.330 --> 00:28:40.920
So the origin of Google
Hangouts was actually Google+.

00:28:40.920 --> 00:28:44.730
So we built that as a
social media solution.

00:28:44.730 --> 00:28:46.770
It was intended
that families could

00:28:46.770 --> 00:28:49.260
meet using Google
Hangouts, or friends

00:28:49.260 --> 00:28:50.910
could catch up even
though they were

00:28:50.910 --> 00:28:52.230
separated by a lot of distance.

00:28:52.230 --> 00:28:56.760
And while that was successful,
what was even more successful

00:28:56.760 --> 00:28:58.930
was businesses using that
to conduct their business.

00:28:58.930 --> 00:29:03.030
So that's what led us to bring
that into the G Suite family.

00:29:03.030 --> 00:29:05.970
And like I said,
that was, again,

00:29:05.970 --> 00:29:08.479
this intention of
taking a consumer tool

00:29:08.479 --> 00:29:10.020
and disrupting the
enterprise space--

00:29:10.020 --> 00:29:12.660
something that's very popular
with technology companies

00:29:12.660 --> 00:29:13.930
these days.

00:29:13.930 --> 00:29:16.740
And what we found was, although
we had a really good experience

00:29:16.740 --> 00:29:19.500
with Hangouts Meet, there were
enterprise features that we

00:29:19.500 --> 00:29:21.990
didn't think about in
that Google+ use case.

00:29:21.990 --> 00:29:25.380
Recording is a great
example of that--

00:29:25.380 --> 00:29:27.885
having that
high-quality experience

00:29:27.885 --> 00:29:29.010
is a great example of that.

00:29:29.010 --> 00:29:31.410
Phone dial-in and
dial-out are examples

00:29:31.410 --> 00:29:33.327
of those enterprise-grade
features that really

00:29:33.327 --> 00:29:35.951
didn't come up when you thought
about building a consumer tool,

00:29:35.951 --> 00:29:37.600
but had to be a part
of the solution.

00:29:37.600 --> 00:29:41.820
So Hangouts Meet really
represents an entire rebuild

00:29:41.820 --> 00:29:44.400
of the architecture
of Google Hangouts.

00:29:44.400 --> 00:29:46.587
And the focus, this
time, is on building

00:29:46.587 --> 00:29:48.170
a tool that's ready
for the enterprise

00:29:48.170 --> 00:29:51.510
and provides that
enterprise-grade experience.

00:29:51.510 --> 00:29:54.010
"Where can I buy
Hangouts Meet hardware?"

00:29:54.010 --> 00:30:00.450
So this is a geography question
as well as a tactical question.

00:30:00.450 --> 00:30:03.060
So Google Hangouts
Meet hardware is

00:30:03.060 --> 00:30:05.790
available in North
America, the EU,

00:30:05.790 --> 00:30:11.690
as well as Japan and Australia.

00:30:11.690 --> 00:30:14.679
We do have ways of importing
into other countries as well.

00:30:14.679 --> 00:30:16.470
And it's really through
our partner network

00:30:16.470 --> 00:30:17.136
that we do that.

00:30:17.136 --> 00:30:20.190
So we actually sell the hardware
through our partner network.

00:30:20.190 --> 00:30:24.709
You can go online on google.com.

00:30:24.709 --> 00:30:26.250
Just search for
Google Hangouts Meet.

00:30:26.250 --> 00:30:28.530
You'll find some places
you can buy online as

00:30:28.530 --> 00:30:30.960
well through the Google Store.

00:30:30.960 --> 00:30:33.860
But at the end the day, we
would recommend reaching out

00:30:33.860 --> 00:30:35.582
to your G Suite partner.

00:30:35.582 --> 00:30:37.290
And typically, they're
going to also sell

00:30:37.290 --> 00:30:39.770
this hardware as well.

00:30:39.770 --> 00:30:41.760
"If I have deployment
questions or need tips,

00:30:41.760 --> 00:30:43.630
are there helpful
resources for me?"

00:30:43.630 --> 00:30:44.950
Absolutely, there are.

00:30:44.950 --> 00:30:47.140
One thing that we've
just soft launched

00:30:47.140 --> 00:30:49.840
is a room design guide.

00:30:49.840 --> 00:30:53.500
We are probably the largest
users of this Google Hangouts

00:30:53.500 --> 00:30:55.300
Meet technology.

00:30:55.300 --> 00:30:58.210
And we've used that experience
and put that into a guide.

00:30:58.210 --> 00:31:01.060
Our own AV team has
actually taken their time

00:31:01.060 --> 00:31:03.850
to build out a
guide that outlines

00:31:03.850 --> 00:31:07.420
the best practices in terms
of designing the room--

00:31:07.420 --> 00:31:09.940
from how you set up the
chairs and how you set up

00:31:09.940 --> 00:31:11.500
your monitors-- to
the hardware you

00:31:11.500 --> 00:31:13.810
need in there, and how you
connect it all together.

00:31:19.830 --> 00:31:24.390
The email address
meeting-room-pod@google.com--

00:31:24.390 --> 00:31:28.140
I'll repeat that again--
meeting-room-pod is a great way

00:31:28.140 --> 00:31:30.810
to reach out to us and get
more information on that,

00:31:30.810 --> 00:31:34.410
as well as there's a whole
host of materials online.

00:31:34.410 --> 00:31:36.910
A best practices guide for
setting up your network--

00:31:36.910 --> 00:31:38.820
that's something to
consider as you decide

00:31:38.820 --> 00:31:41.380
you need to become a
videoconference-enabled company

00:31:41.380 --> 00:31:43.830
is how do you make sure
your network can handle

00:31:43.830 --> 00:31:44.616
that extra load?

00:31:44.616 --> 00:31:45.990
We've got a lot
of best practices

00:31:45.990 --> 00:31:49.260
on optimizing that network,
setting it up, and how to have

00:31:49.260 --> 00:31:52.560
a great experience with that.

00:31:52.560 --> 00:31:54.810
So with that, that's the
end of the questions.

00:31:54.810 --> 00:31:56.850
I want to thank
everyone for watching.

00:31:56.850 --> 00:31:59.400
And please tune in next
Tuesday for the next session

00:31:59.400 --> 00:32:01.147
of "Google Cloud OnAir."

00:32:01.147 --> 00:32:01.980
Thank you very much.

00:32:01.980 --> 00:32:03.530
Have a great day, everybody.

00:32:03.530 --> 00:32:06.280
[MUSIC PLAYING]

